
For this week, our team accomplished two major tasks. First, the container for the sensor was 3D printed. We tested it with the Arduino,
and it functioned perfectly. On top of that, we worked on integrating the Arduino with the AS7265X spectral sensor to detect different colours.
The sensor provides 18 channels of light reflection data, some of which directly correspond to wavelengths associated with red, green, and blue light.

   Because the sensor does not output colour values directly, we developed a straightforward method to determine the sensed colour. We focused on 6 specific
channels: one used to identify black (where we noticed a tracking issue),  three dedicated to calculating green values, one for the red value and one for 
the blue value. The green spectrum requires higher precision because its readings are easily skewed by red or blue reflections. By comparing these channels 
to find which output is highest, the system identifies the target colour. We then linked each colour to a specific audio file so that sensor inputs trigger
audio playback in real time, successfully using light reflection as a control variable for sound.

We also identified a few key issues to address in the coming weeks:
1.	Black light detection: The sensor currently struggles to detect black, as interference from other spectral channels distorts the readings.
2.	Audio playback: The audio transitions are choppy, so we need a method to crossfade or harmonize the samples for smoother playback.
3.	Sample scaling and logic: The final product will feature many more samples derived from a 3-minute audio track split across the 18 channels.
We are developing a system to handle this playback logic. For example, if green is detected, the system determines which of the three green sub-channels 
has the highest value and then plays the audio that is directly linked to that channel. We plan to keep expanding this logic to create a more dynamic
interactive experience.

